    ],
    "raw_text": [
      "{\"predicted_cves\": [\"CVE-2017-13031\"], \"predicted_files\": [\"print-frag6.c\"], \"predicted_functions\": [\"frag6_print\"], \"root_cause\": \"In frag6_print (print-frag6.c:43-55), ND_TCHECK(dp->ip6f_offlg) guards only the fragment header's offset field at offset 2 within struct ip6_frag. However, the length computation at lines 48-50 and 53-55 reads ip6->ip6_plen (IPv6 payload-length at bp2+4) via EXTRACT_16BITS(&ip6->ip6_plen) without any corresponding ND_TCHECK guard on the IPv6 header. If a packet snapshot is crafted such that the fragment header falls at the boundary of the capture buffer while the IPv6 header's payload-length field lies outside it, this yields an out-of-bounds read.\", \"evidence\": \"print-frag6.c line 43: ND_TCHECK(dp->ip6f_offlg) bounds-checks only the fragment header at bp. Lines 48-50: sizeof(struct ip6_hdr) + EXTRACT_16BITS(&ip6->ip6_plen) - (long)(bp - bp2) - sizeof(struct ip6_frag) reads ip6->ip6_plen at bp2+4 without any TCHECK guard. The poc pcap triggers this code path: tcpdump output shows 'next-header Fragment (44)' and '[|frag]' confirming frag6_print is reached and the missing-guard truncation logic is exercised. The caller in print-ip6.c:307 passes cp (fragment header) and ip6 (IPv6 header) to frag6_print, and only cp < ndo->ndo_snapend is checked in the caller's loop condition, with no additional guard on bp2-relative accesses inside the callee.\", \"confidence\": \"high\"}"
